489
00:22:51.080 --> 00:22:52.560
It was a series on television.

490
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.860
Which was written by two of the office writers.

491
00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:56.120
Oh, that's right.

492
00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:56.720
That's right.

493
00:22:56.820 --> 00:22:57.140
That's right.

494
00:22:57.500 --> 00:22:57.880
I and Jane.

495
00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:02.140
Jenna, you could just have a life pivot where you become a detective.

496
00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.640
Well, Angela, we want to be mom detectives.

497
00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.240
Maybe this is part of why.

498
00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:08.020
This is a mom detectives.

499
00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:08.800
That's a great show.

500
00:23:09.080 --> 00:23:09.200
Yeah.

501
00:23:09.320 --> 00:23:09.880
That's a great show.

502
00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:10.580
Thank you.

503
00:23:10.580 --> 00:23:10.980
Thank you.

504
00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:11.560
We think so.

505
00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:13.440
We're solving crimes no one cares about.

506
00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:14.780
And we always have snacks.

507
00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:17.020
And we almost never solve it.

508
00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:17.800
Endless snacks.

509
00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:21.180
At the end of every episode, our tagline is, we may never know.

510
00:23:21.220 --> 00:23:22.020
We may never know.

511
00:23:22.560 --> 00:23:22.940
That's it.

512
00:23:23.140 --> 00:23:24.700
There's no payoff, Craig.

513
00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:25.140
Sorry.

514
00:23:25.260 --> 00:23:27.160
There's a lot of setup with no payoff.

515
00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:27.620
Believe me.

516
00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:28.820
Just stretch it out.

517
00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:30.240
We did nine years of stretching it out.

518
00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:43.460
I want to talk a little bit about how you guys shot the show.

519
00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:48.280
Because The Office and How I Met Your Mother, they both started around the same time.

520
00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.080
And I feel like both of our shows did a new kind of thing.

521
00:23:51.260 --> 00:23:57.340
Like The Office did the whole documentary style, messy camera work kind of spy shot thing.

522
00:23:58.080 --> 00:23:58.940
No audience.

523
00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:00.500
Yeah, no audience.

524
00:24:00.500 --> 00:24:06.900
Your show was shot in the three-camera style, but was not filmed in front of an audience.

525
00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:09.400
But it had a laugh track.

526
00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:12.600
But also, you did some single-camera stuff.

527
00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:14.060
How did it all work?

528
00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:19.380
Yeah, it was, we created some, Carter and I, Carter is co-creator of the show with me.

529
00:24:19.540 --> 00:24:21.600
We really didn't know what we were doing.

530
00:24:22.160 --> 00:24:22.980
That's the biggest thing.

531
00:24:23.080 --> 00:24:25.540
We stumbled in really not knowing how to do this.

532
00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:28.920
We weren't like these grizzled old like sitcom hacks kind of guys.

533
00:24:28.920 --> 00:24:30.840
We had come from like late night television.

534
00:24:30.860 --> 00:24:34.200
We worked on an animated show, a couple short-lived sitcoMs.

535
00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.480
We really had never written a multi-camera sitcom.

536
00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:40.180
So we wrote this pilot that we thought was great because it moved.

537
00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:41.280
It was lots of short scenes.

538
00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:41.860
It popped around.

539
00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:42.640
It played with time.

540
00:24:43.080 --> 00:24:44.460
And we gave it to our producer.

541
00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:48.660
And she was like, this is unproducible in front of an audience from the human beings.

542
00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:53.220
Like, this is like how you, we hope you understand that you cannot do this like friends.

543
00:24:53.380 --> 00:24:57.640
We're like, can't we just keep the audience for a long time and do a lot of pre-shoots?

544
00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:00.680
And they were like, you will have, it will be a hostage situation.

545
00:25:00.840 --> 00:25:02.560
Like, the audience will just be there for three days.

546
00:25:02.720 --> 00:25:03.740
They'll be trying to escape.

547
00:25:04.060 --> 00:25:07.360
Like, it's like this, you have to break this down in a different way.

548
00:25:08.060 --> 00:25:12.260
And Pam Freiman and Cissy Greenberg, our brilliant director and producer who did the entire series,

549
00:25:12.440 --> 00:25:16.940
really came up with a way to, over three days, shoot the show,

550
00:25:16.940 --> 00:25:20.640
kind of to look like a sitcom and kind of to look at times like a single count.

551
00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:21.720
We went out onto New York Street.

552
00:25:21.840 --> 00:25:22.360
We went on locations.

553
00:25:22.520 --> 00:25:25.580
We did lots of fun sort of cinematic storytelling.

554
00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:31.040
And we really let ourselves think about it as not just a half-hour sitcom on CBS, which again, we were.

555
00:25:31.460 --> 00:25:33.740
Our lead-in was King of Queens when we launched.

556
00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:35.640
And our lead-out was Two and a Half Men.

557
00:25:36.180 --> 00:25:38.100
And we were such a weird black sheep.

558
00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:41.840
Our numbers were not nearly as big as the show before us and the show after us,

559
00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:42.940
which means you're the dip.

560
00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:43.760
You're like the hammock.

561
00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:47.800
You're bringing the whole ratings of the night down.

562
00:25:48.120 --> 00:25:49.820
Oh, we're familiar with that.

563
00:25:49.920 --> 00:25:51.400
We were that as well.

564
00:25:51.520 --> 00:25:52.200
Oh, yeah.

565
00:25:52.360 --> 00:25:53.520
You know that move.

566
00:25:53.740 --> 00:25:58.420
But we were great in 18 to 34, which CBS was looking for.

567
00:25:58.580 --> 00:26:00.720
They were not doing great in that younger category.

568
00:26:00.800 --> 00:26:07.060
And we eventually, over those first few years, started to become their number one show in 18 to 34.

569
00:26:07.060 --> 00:26:14.560
And they realized, well, not as many people are watching this as watch these other shows, but more young people are watching this than watch those other shows.

570
00:26:14.700 --> 00:26:16.380
And that was our survival.

571
00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:20.380
Well, I love how it shot, even now.

572
00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:26.340
And I think that's one of the reasons, if I were to guess, why it, quote-unquote, holds up so well.

573
00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:27.860
You know?

574
00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:28.240
Yeah.

575
00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:31.120
You were a little ahead of your time in that way.

576
00:26:31.500 --> 00:26:42.340
Brian Eno, you know, the ambient music pioneer Brian Eno, he has this thing about how new forms are created when the old form can't contain what the bigger expression is.

577
00:26:42.340 --> 00:26:48.100
So, for instance, distortion in a guitar was because the amps got blown out.

578
00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:51.800
And then it became the sound that everyone was actually chasing.

579
00:26:52.120 --> 00:26:57.540
So, now you have, like, you're trying to mimic the sound of blowing out an amp.

580
00:26:57.960 --> 00:26:59.740
And that becomes a whole new genre.

581
00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:03.240
But it was actually a kind of mistake or something that couldn't contain it.

582
00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:09.040
So, it's almost like Carter and Craig's naivete actually created a new form.

583
00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:09.720
Yeah.

584
00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:11.780
Because they kind of didn't know any better.

585
00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:12.060
We didn't know what we were doing.

586
00:27:12.240 --> 00:27:13.280
We didn't know we couldn't do it.

587
00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:13.480
Yeah.

588
00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:17.420
And I feel like I saw it in other things, too, after watching your show.

589
00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:24.340
Especially, like, when you would have a character say something and then it would cut to a different time to the other person reacting.

590
00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:24.900
You know?

591
00:27:25.040 --> 00:27:31.140
Like, you would sort of set up, like, Ted would say something and then it would cut to Robin's reaction to Lily or things like that.

592
00:27:31.300 --> 00:27:33.300
And it zipped along so fun.

593
00:27:33.420 --> 00:27:35.160
And I feel like I've seen that in movies.

594
00:27:35.160 --> 00:27:38.600
But I feel like the first time I saw it really was watching your show.

595
00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:39.360
Yeah.

596
00:27:40.220 --> 00:27:42.060
Most multi-cameras don't move that way.

597
00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:43.400
I love that The Office moves that way.

598
00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:46.360
I feel like we should say, like, I'm a huge fan of The Office.

599
00:27:46.500 --> 00:27:48.100
I watched every single episode of The Office.

600
00:27:48.200 --> 00:27:48.900
I watch it door-to-door.

601
00:27:49.080 --> 00:27:50.160
I'm such a huge fan.

602
00:27:50.460 --> 00:27:52.100
I wanted to ask all of you this question.

603
00:27:52.180 --> 00:27:52.600
You too, Josh.

604
00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:54.540
Because I don't know this, the answer for you.

605
00:27:55.020 --> 00:28:03.420
Were you guys able to, while you were on TV, while you were on The Office, you come, you're shooting The Office, you come home, do you watch other sitcoms?

606
00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:10.000
And I'm honored, Jenna, that you were watching How I Met Your Mother, I guess, which I never really knew that you were at that time until now.

607
00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:12.360
I would come home from working on How I Met Your Mother all day.

608
00:28:12.600 --> 00:28:15.640
And the only show, the only comedy I would watch is The Office.

609
00:28:16.020 --> 00:28:18.540
I love The Office, and my wife and I watch every episode.

610
00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:22.900
I could not watch any other comedies because my brain would start to compare to them.

611
00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:24.600
Ah, they're a bigger hit than us.

612
00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:25.900
Or, ah, we're better than that.

